window.docContents[174].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/Harmonic-Functions-and-Model-Kernels/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Harmonic-Functions-and-Model-Kernels--The-Newtonian-Pole":{"contents":"For , the power-law kernel has the same point-source normalization as\nthe logarithmic kernel. We recover the point source by spherical integration:\nthe normalized radial flux gives the value of a test function at the origin.\n\nLet . For every compactly supported  test function\n,Consequently the regular distribution induced by the Newtonian kernel is a\nfundamental solution of the negative Laplacian:\n\nWrite  for the spherical mean of  and  for the\nintegral of  over . The ball divergence theorem\nexpresses  through . The normalized kernel flux then makes the\nspherical integral of  the derivative of\n. This primitive tends to  as  and\nvanishes beyond the support of . Coarea and the fundamental theorem\nof calculus give the displayed identity. Applying it to smooth test functions\nidentifies the negative distributional Laplacian with .\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Harmonic Functions and Model Kernels","header":"5.2.4. The Newtonian Pole","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/Harmonic-Functions-and-Model-Kernels/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Harmonic-Functions-and-Model-Kernels--The-Newtonian-Pole"},"/Foundations/Operators___-solution-concepts___-and-weak-formulations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Operators___-solution-concepts___-and-weak-formulations":{"contents":"The formula  in\n combines a choice of coordinate derivatives\nwith assumptions under which they exist. We fix those operators and state the\nregularity needed to interpret them classically.\n\nThe starting point is the Fréchet derivative, the linear first-order\napproximation that carries the chain rule, the inverse function theorem, and\nvariational calculus in normed spaces. Out of that single notion we build the\ngradient, divergence, and Laplacian as finite coordinate operations, so that\n\" is harmonic\" unfolds all the way down to iterated Fréchet\nderivatives along basis directions. The\nverified computer algebra system of the computation chapter uses the same\nconventions. Asking it for the Laplacian of the model harmonic polynomial\n\n-- Check the planar Laplacian using the\n-- coordinate conventions fixed below.\n#cas [laplacian x, y] (x ^ 2 - y ^ 2)\n\n\nproduces a checked zero residual, rendered from the compiler's own output:\n\n#cas [laplacian x, y]\nDomain: (x, y) ∈ ℝ^2\nInput (scalar ℝ): (x ^ 2 - y ^ 2)\nOutput (scalar ℝ): 0\n\n[CAS.Command.details] More details\n\n\nThe calculation certifies  for the same coordinate\nLaplacian used in the later analytic statements.\n\nOn the unit disk, this polynomial is harmonic but does not solve the\nzero-boundary Dirichlet problem: its boundary values at  and\n are  and . On the circle\n, its boundary datum is\n. The residual calculation verifies the interior equation;\nthe boundary calculation determines which Dirichlet problem it solves.\n\nIn a polynomial heat solution,\nthe spatial Laplacian instead balances a time derivative.\n\nA residual can vanish at every point while the boundary condition is\nstill unproved. A derivative term exists in Lean whether or not the function is\ndifferentiable, because fderiv is a total function. A distributional identity\ncan hold without a locally integrable representative. The solution predicates\nstate separately which identities and regularity conditions are required.\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations","header":"3.1. Operators, solution concepts, and weak formulations","id":"/Foundations/Operators___-solution-concepts___-and-weak-formulations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Operators___-solution-concepts___-and-weak-formulations"},"/Foundations/PDE-Problems-and-Differential-Operators/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--PDE-Problems-and-Differential-Operators--Cauchy-problems":{"contents":"A Cauchy problem prescribes a solution and some of its derivatives on a\nhypersurface  and asks the equation to do the rest. For an equation\nof order  the natural data are the values of  and its first \nnormal derivatives along . Two questions come before any existence\ntheorem. Do these data, together with the equation, pin down every derivative\nof  on ? And if they do, does the resulting Taylor series\nconverge?\n\nThe one-dimensional wave equation  shows how the first\nquestion can fail. On the line  the data are  and\n. Differentiating them in  gives , , and\n on the line, and the equation then supplies the one missing\nsecond derivative, . On the line  the same procedure\nbreaks down. Differentiating the first derivatives along the line gives\n and , hence their difference\n. The equation prescribes exactly that difference, so it\neither repeats what the data already say or contradicts them, and in neither\ncase does it determine the three second derivatives.\nThe difference is visible in the principal symbol: for a normal\n it is , which equals  for\n and vanishes for . A hypersurface is\nnoncharacteristic when the principal symbol is nonzero on its normals.\n\nEven when every derivative is determined, the Taylor series need not\nconverge. Kovalevskaya's example is the heat equation  with\n. The line  is characteristic for the heat\noperator, whose principal symbol  vanishes at , but\nthe equation is solved for , so every time derivative is still\ndetermined formally: . At the origin\n, so the formal solution is\n\n\n\nwhose terms grow roughly like  and diverge for every , although\n is analytic on the whole real line. The wave equation with the same\ndatum and  has the solution\n, and at  its series\n converges for .\n\n\n\nThe figure plots both series. The wave partial sums settle onto\n inside the unit interval, while at each plotted time the size of\nthe -th term of the heat series first shrinks and then grows without\nbound. The Cauchy–Kovalevskaya theorem says that the divergence cannot happen\nfor a first-order system solved for its time derivative with analytic\ncoefficients and data.  treats\nnoncharacteristic surfaces in §4.6.1 and the analytic theorem in §4.6.3.\nThe determination of derivatives is proved for smooth solutions of a\nquasilinear scalar equation, with no analyticity assumption. The convergence\nproof runs on\nMathlib's multivariable formal power series, with the formal solution\nconstructed by recursion in the time degree, over any commutative\n-algebra, before any estimate is made.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009PDE Problems and Differential Operators","header":"3.2.4. Cauchy problems","id":"/Foundations/PDE-Problems-and-Differential-Operators/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--PDE-Problems-and-Differential-Operators--Cauchy-problems"},"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Transport-and-Classical-Waves/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Transport-and-Classical-Waves--Nonlinear-Wave-Equations--A-classical-wave-from-bounded-scalar-data":{"contents":"Consider  with displacement  and\nvelocity . The reaction is globally Lipschitz and the\ninitial profiles are bounded and smooth. Their derivatives need no uniform\nbound, and  is allowed. In one dimension the characteristic\nintegral constructs a classical solution from these data for every\nnonnegative time.\n\nLet  be the free d'Alembert wave with data . The same\nzero-data Duhamel operator used in the linear chapter takes, for ,\nthe form\n\n\n\nThe nonlinear equation  asks us to solve\n. On a finite strip ,\nbounded continuous fields form a complete space in the supremum norm.\nContinuity here is joint in time and space. The estimate\n keeps the source bounded, and\n keeps its Duhamel field bounded.\n\nFor bounded continuous , a globally\n-Lipschitz , and every finite , there is a unique\nbounded continuous solution  of  on\n. If , restricting  to\n gives .\n\nWrite . Its Lipschitz constant is at most\n, which need not be less than one. Repeated time integration\ngives the stronger estimateFor each fixed , the coefficient tends to zero as .\nSome iterate therefore contracts, giving a unique fixed point of\n on that whole strip. There is no small-time restriction on\n. The value at time  uses source values only between  and\n, so restriction to a shorter strip still solves its integral\nequation. Uniqueness identifies the restricted solution.\n\nThe integral solution is initially only continuous. To get two derivatives,\nwe insert it into the Duhamel formula twice. Extend  by clipping\nonly its time argument to , and call this continuous extension\n. Define\n\n\n\nWith  and , the free field  is .\nThe one-dimensional Duhamel operator sends continuous forcing to a jointly\n field, so  is . If , its composition\nwith  is , and the linear Duhamel regularity theorem makes\n jointly . Causality identifies both insertions with\n on the closed forward strip. This proves regularity of the actual\nfixed point from ordinary scalar regularity of the data and reaction.\n\nLet  be  and globally Lipschitz,\nand let  and  have\nbounded ranges. There is a field  such thatBoth prescribed values are joint spacetime traces:For every finite , the field is bounded on\n; the bound may depend on .\nNo integrability, smallness, vanishing condition on , or bound on\nthe derivatives of  is assumed.\n\nThe compatible strip solutions define one field for all forward times.\nNear any positive time, choose a fixed larger horizon. Compatibility makes\nthe field agree on that neighborhood with the  second insertion\nfor this horizon. Near time zero, use the signed-time extension from the\nunit strip. These local representations prove global  regularity;\nthe nonlinear PDE is asserted for nonnegative time.The Duhamel field has zero initial displacement and velocity, so subtracting\nit from  retains both assigned initial values. Continuity of \nand  then gives the two joint traces. On every finite forward\nstrip the field agrees with the bounded continuous fixed point, giving the\nstated horizon-dependent bound.\n\nThe uniqueness assertion in this construction concerns bounded continuous\nsolutions of the integral equation on each finite strip. The raw classical\nendpoint above states existence; comparisons between general classical\nfields use the energy and cone arguments below. Global forward existence\nalso allows growth in time: for a constant reaction  and\nzero initial data, the constructed field is  for\n.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Transport and Classical Waves\u0009Nonlinear Wave Equations","header":"7.2.4.3. A classical wave from bounded scalar data","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Transport-and-Classical-Waves/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Transport-and-Classical-Waves--Nonlinear-Wave-Equations--A-classical-wave-from-bounded-scalar-data"}});